P5.4 — load and burst against the candidate
specs/readiness-recovery-10-10/tasks.md P5.4

date (UTC): 2026-08-02
commit:     110e766 (+ the ml-inference limiter fix this run produced)
host:       Darwin arm64, 14 cores
k6:         v2.0.0
targets:    platform-api 127.0.0.1:8188, ml-inference 127.0.0.1:8090

READ THIS BEFORE THE NUMBERS
  platform-api is a DEBUG build here. These latencies are a regression floor-check, NOT a capacity
  statement about a production candidate — P5.1 (approved SLOs and a capacity model) is still open,
  and there is no approved target to compare against.

  Consecutive k6 runs against the SAME ml-inference process SHARE the limiter's 60-second sliding
  window. A run that starts seconds after a heavy one inherits its spent budget. That is not a bug;
  it is why run 2 below reads worse than its own load would explain, and it is recorded so nobody
  reads these three lines as three independent measurements.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
WHAT THE RUNS SAY

  1 VU, 15s        error_rate 0.000   635 requests
                   p95: health 1.4ms · surahs 18.0ms · alignment 1.7ms · tajweed 1.0ms
                   Every endpoint healthy. This is the run that says the SERVICE works.

  10 VUs, 30s      error_rate 0.342   13,750 requests  (~27,500 req/min, ML share ~11,000/min)
                   p95: health 3.8ms · surahs 5.7ms · alignment 4.4ms · tajweed 2.5ms
                   The errors are the ML limiter answering 429, not the service failing: the load
                   generated is roughly 1.8x the configured trusted ceiling. Latency stays flat while
                   it happens, which is the limiter doing its job rather than the service degrading.

  50 VUs burst     error_rate 0.666   51,525 requests
                   p95: health 4.7ms · surahs 3.6ms · alignment 3.9ms · tajweed 2.6ms
                   Same story, further past the ceiling. Nothing crashed, no latency cliff, no
                   interrupted iterations in any run.

  k6's own thresholds FAIL on all three multi-VU runs, and that is correct — they assert
  `errors < 1%`, and a run deliberately driven past the rate limiter cannot meet it. The thresholds
  are not adjusted to make the run green; a threshold moved to fit the measurement has stopped
  measuring.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
WHAT THIS RUN FOUND — a production ceiling, not a test artifact

  ml-inference limited requests at 100/minute PER IP. Every learner's analysis reaches it through
  platform-api's proxy, and platform-api does not forward `x-forwarded-for` — so from ml-inference's
  side, all traffic from all learners in all tenants arrives from ONE address and shares ONE bucket.

  The whole platform was capped at 100 ML requests per minute. A class of twenty children practising
  would have 429ed each other.

  Demonstrated directly rather than inferred: with the single shared bucket restored as a mutation,
  services/ml-inference/rate-limit.test.mjs reports 105 of 105 authenticated requests rejected.

  FIXED: two budgets. Callers holding the server-side ML_API_KEY (platform-api, agents) get
  ML_TRUSTED_RATE_LIMIT_MAX (default 6000/min); everyone else keeps the 100/min anti-abuse budget,
  in a separate namespace so an unauthenticated flood cannot spend the trusted allowance. A ceiling
  rather than an exemption: if the key leaks, "authenticated" stops meaning "trustworthy".

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
WHAT WAS **NOT** RUN — P5.4 stays OPEN

  P5.4 names load, burst, long-audio, reconnect, timeout, duplicate-delivery, partial-loss and
  recovery. Load and burst are above. The rest are not done, and a row that closed on two of eight
  would be a false claim:

  - reconnect / partial-loss: scripts/chaos-realtime-reconnect.mjs exists and needs the
    realtime-gateway built with REALTIME_CHAOS_* armed. Not run here.
  - long-audio: untested. The audit's concern is concrete — transcribeSession uses synchronous
    readFileSync per chunk and one Buffer.concat over the whole session, so a long recitation is a
    single large allocation on the event loop.
  - timeout, duplicate-delivery, recovery: no harness exists yet.

  And the standing precondition: P5.1 has to define SLOs, a capacity model and RTO/RPO before any of
  these numbers can be called pass or fail. Right now they are measurements without a target.
